Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the Several Opinions on the Acceptance and Review of Civil Retrial Applications

Fa Fa [2009] No. 26

To the High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; and the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

In order to protect the parties' right to apply for a retrial in accordance with the law and to standardize the work of the People's Courts in accepting and reviewing civil retrial applications, the Supreme People's Court has formulated the Several Opinions on the Acceptance and Review of Civil Retrial Applications, which are hereby issued to you for conscientious implementation in light of actual trial work.

April 27, 2009

Several Opinions on the Acceptance and Review of Civil Retrial Applications

In order to protect the parties' right to apply for a retrial in accordance with the law and to standardize the work of the People's Courts in accepting and reviewing civil retrial applications, and in accordance with the relevant provisions of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China and the Interpretation of the Supreme People's Court on Several Issues Concerning the Application of the Trial Supervision Procedure of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and in light of the actual trial work, the following opinions are hereby put forward regarding the acceptance and review of civil retrial applications:

I. Acceptance of Civil Retrial Applications

Article 1 A party or a person not party to the case applying for a retrial shall submit materials such as a retrial application and shall also submit copies of the retrial application in accordance with the number of the respondent and other parties in the original instance.

Article 2 The People's Court shall examine whether the retrial application indicates the following matters:

(1) The basic information of the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original instance. If a party is a natural person, the name, sex, age, ethnicity, occupation, work unit, domicile, and valid contact telephone number and mailing address shall be listed; if a party is a legal person or any other organization, the name, domicile, and the name and position of the legal representative or principal responsible person, as well as the valid contact telephone number and mailing address, shall be listed;

(2) The name of the court of the original instance, and the case number of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation document;

(3) Specific retrial requests;

(4) The statutory grounds for applying for a retrial and the specific facts and reasons;

(5) The name of the court accepting the retrial application;

(6) The signature or seal of the applicant for retrial.

Article 3 When applying for a retrial, the applicant for retrial shall, in addition to submitting a retrial application that complies with the provisions of the preceding article, also submit the following materials:

(1) If the applicant for retrial is a natural person, a copy of the identification document shall be submitted; if the applicant for retrial is a legal person or any other organization, a copy of the business license and a certificate of identity of the legal representative or principal responsible person shall be submitted. If another person is entrusted to file the application, a power of attorney and the agent's identification document shall be submitted;

(2) The original effective judgment or ruling document for which retrial is sought, or a verified accurate copy; if the effective judgment is from a second instance or retrial, the original judgment or ruling documents of the first instance and the second instance, or verified accurate copies thereof, shall also be submitted simultaneously;

(3) Copies of the main evidence submitted during the litigation process in the original instance;

(4) Evidentiary materials supporting the grounds for retrial and the retrial claims.

Article 4 When submitting materials such as the retrial application, the applicant for retrial shall submit a material list in duplicate, and may attach an electronic version of the retrial application materials, and simultaneously fill in a confirmation form for the service address.

Article 5 If the materials submitted by the applicant for retrial, such as the retrial application, do not meet the above requirements, or contain personal attacks or other content that may escalate conflicts, the People's Court shall return the materials to the applicant for retrial and inform them to supplement or correct them.

If the materials such as the retrial application meet the above requirements, the People's Court shall indicate the date of receipt on the material list submitted by the applicant for retrial, affix the receiving stamp, and return one copy of the list to the applicant for retrial.

Article 6 Where the retrial application filed by the applicant for retrial meets the following conditions, the People's Court shall accept the application within 5 days and send a notice of acceptance to the applicant for retrial, and at the same time send a notice of acceptance, a copy of the retrial application, and a confirmation form for the service address to the respondent and other parties in the original instance:

(1) The applicant for retrial is a party listed in the effective judgment or ruling document, or is a person not party to the case as provided for by law and judicial interpretation;

(2) The court accepting the retrial application is the court at the next higher level than the court that rendered the effective judgment;

(3) The judgment for which retrial is sought is an effective judgment that law and judicial interpretation permit to be retried;

(4) The grounds for applying for a retrial fall within the circumstances provided for in Article 179 of the Civil Procedure Law.

If the retrial application does not meet the above conditions, the applicant for retrial shall be promptly informed.

Article 7 If the applicant for retrial applies for a retrial to the court of the original instance, the court of the original instance shall provide a proper explanation in relation to the grounds for retrial and in combination with the reasons for the original judgment. If the applicant for retrial persists in applying for a retrial, they shall be informed that they may apply to the court at the next higher level.

Article 8 If the applicant for retrial applies for a retrial to a court more than one level above, the relevant higher-level court shall inform them to apply to the court at the next higher level above the court of the original instance.

Article 9 If the People's Court considers that the retrial application does not meet the time limit requirement provided for in Article 184 of the Civil Procedure Law, it shall inform the applicant for retrial. If the applicant for retrial considers that the statutory time limit has not been exceeded, the People's Court may set a deadline requiring them to submit a copy of the service receipt of the effective judgment or ruling document, or other corresponding evidentiary materials capable of proving the actual effective date of the judgment or ruling document.

II. Review of Civil Retrial Applications

Article 10 After accepting a retrial application, the People's Court shall form a collegial panel to conduct the review.

Article 11 The People's Court, when reviewing a retrial application, shall focus on whether the grounds for retrial raised by the applicant are established; grounds not asserted by the applicant for retrial shall not be reviewed.

Article 12 The People's Court, when reviewing a retrial application, shall examine changes in the litigation subject qualifications of the parties.

Article 13 The People's Court shall adopt the following methods when reviewing a retrial application:

(1) Examining the materials submitted by the parties, such as the retrial application and written opinions;

(2) Reviewing the case files of the original instance;

(3) Questioning the parties;

(4) Organizing a hearing for the parties.

Article 14 If, after examining the materials submitted by the applicant for retrial, such as the retrial application, the written opinions submitted by the opposing party, the original judgment or ruling documents, and evidence, the People's Court is sufficiently certain that the grounds for retrial cannot be established, it may directly render a ruling to dismiss the retrial application.

Article 15 For cases where a retrial is applied for on the following grounds and the grounds for retrial are sufficiently certain to be established based on the material submitted by the parties, the People's Court may directly render a ruling to initiate a retrial:

(1) There is a violation of legal provisions resulting in erroneous jurisdiction;

(2) The composition of the trial organization is unlawful, or a trial person who should have recused themselves according to law did not do so;

(3) A person without capacity for litigation acted without a statutory agent, or a party who should have participated in the litigation did not participate due to reasons not attributable to themselves or their litigation agent;

(4) The legal document on which the original judgment or ruling was based has been revoked or modified;

(5) A trial person engaged in acts of corruption, bribery, favoritism for personal gain, or perversion of the law in adjudicating the case, as confirmed by relevant criminal legal documents or disciplinary sanction decisions.

Article 16 If the People's Court decides to transfer the files for review, the court of the original instance shall submit the case files as required within 15 days after receiving the notice of file transfer request.

The scope of the transferred case files of the original instance may be determined based on the needs of the review work. When necessary, and provided authenticity is guaranteed, the court of the original instance may be requested to promptly submit relevant case file materials by means such as fax, copies, or electronic documents.

Article 17 The People's Court may, based on the needs of the review work, question one or both parties.

Article 18 The People's Court may organize a hearing for the parties in cases where a retrial is applied for on the following grounds:

(1) There is new evidence sufficient to overturn the original judgment or ruling;

(2) The basic facts found in the original judgment or ruling lack evidentiary support;

(3) The main evidence on which the facts found in the original judgment or ruling were based is forged;

(4) The application of law in the original judgment or ruling is indeed erroneous.

Article 19 In a case where the collegial panel decides to hold a hearing, the parties shall be notified 5 days before the hearing.

Article 20 The hearing shall be presided over by the presiding judge and shall focus on whether the grounds for retrial are established.

Article 21 If the applicant for retrial, after being summoned by subpoena, refuses to participate in the questioning or hearing without justifiable reasons, or leaves midway without permission, the application for retrial shall be deemed withdrawn by ruling. If the respondent or other parties in the original instance do not participate in the questioning or hearing, or leave midway without permission, they shall be deemed to have waived the right to state their opinions during the questioning or hearing process.

Article 22 In the course of reviewing the retrial application, if the respondent or another party in the original instance submits a qualifying retrial application, they shall be listed as an applicant for retrial, and their grounds for retrial shall be reviewed together, with the review period recalculated. If, upon review, the grounds for retrial asserted by one of the applicants for retrial are established, the People's Court shall immediately render a ruling to initiate a retrial. If the grounds for retrial asserted by all applicants for retrial are not established, all applications shall be dismissed by a single ruling.

Article 23 If, during the review process, the applicant for retrial withdraws the retrial application, the People's Court shall decide by ruling whether to permit the withdrawal.

Article 24 If, during the review process, the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original instance voluntarily reach a settlement agreement, and the parties apply to the People's Court for a mediation document, and it can be determined that the grounds for retrial are established, the People's Court shall render a ruling to initiate a retrial and prepare a mediation document.

Article 25 If, during the review process, the applicant for retrial or the respondent dies or is terminated, the following treatment shall be applied respectively:

(1) If the applicant for retrial has a successor in rights and obligations and that successor applies to participate in the review procedure, the successor shall be substituted as the applicant for retrial;

(2) If the respondent has a successor in rights and obligations, the successor in rights and obligations shall be substituted as the respondent;

(3) If the applicant for retrial has no successor in rights and obligations or their successor in rights and obligations does not apply to participate in the review procedure, the review procedure shall be terminated by ruling;

(4) If the respondent has no successor in rights and obligations and has no property available for enforcement, the review procedure shall be terminated by ruling.

Article 26 If, upon review, the People's Court finds that the retrial application exceeds the period provided for in Article 184 of the Civil Procedure Law, it shall render a ruling to dismiss the application.

Article 27 If, upon review, the People's Court finds that the grounds for retrial are established, the case shall generally be retried by the court itself by way of trial de novo.

Article 28 For the following cases reviewed by the Supreme People's Court or a High People's Court, the court of the original instance may be directed to conduct a retrial:

(1) Cases where a retrial is initiated based on the grounds set forth in Subparagraphs (8) to (13) of Paragraph 1 of Article 179 of the Civil Procedure Law;

(2) Cases where a retrial is initiated due to a violation of statutory procedure that may affect the correctness of the judgment or ruling;

(3) Other cases that the court at the next higher level considers should be directed to the court of the original instance for retrial.

Article 29 The ruling document for retrial by way of trial de novo or for directing a retrial shall include the following contents:

(1) The basic information of the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original instance;

(2) The name of the court of the original instance, the name and case number of the effective judgment or ruling document for which retrial is sought;

(3) The legal basis for the ruling to initiate a retrial;

(4) The outcome of the ruling.

The ruling document shall be signed by the president and affixed with the seal of the People's Court.

Article 30 The ruling document dismissing the retrial application shall include the following contents:

(1) The basic information of the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original instance;

(2) The name of the court of the original instance, the name and case number of the effective judgment or ruling document for which retrial is sought;

(3) The grounds for retrial asserted by the applicant for retrial and the opinions of the respondent;

(4) The reasons for dismissing the retrial application and the legal basis;

(5) The outcome of the ruling.

The ruling document shall be signed by the trial personnel and the clerk, and affixed with the seal of the People's Court.

Article 31 After the retrial application has been dismissed by ruling, if the applicant for retrial again applies for a retrial on the same grounds, it shall not be handled as a retrial application case for review.

If the applicant for retrial, dissatisfied with the ruling dismissing their retrial application, applies for a retrial to the court at the next higher level of the court that made the dismissal ruling, it shall not be handled as a retrial application case for review.

Article 32 The People's Court shall complete the review within 3 months from the date of accepting the retrial application, provided that the period for appraisal, etc., shall not be counted in the review period. If there are special circumstances requiring an extension, approval shall be obtained from the president of the court.

Article 33 Cases accepted before April 1, 2008, that have not yet been concluded and meet the conditions for applying for a retrial shall continue to be reviewed and disposed of by the People's Court that accepted the retrial application and a ruling shall be made.
